{{seealso|Mackerras pendulum for the Australian federal election, 2016}}

Several research and polling firms conduct polls in relation to voting intentions during the parliamentary term and in the lead-up to the [[Australian federal election, 2016|2016 Australian federal election]] on 2 July. Some of the firms also ask voters whether they are satisfied or dissatisfied with the performance of the leaders of the two main parties, as well as who would be the preferred prime minister. Most firms use the flow of preferences at the previous election to determine the [[two-party-preferred vote]]; others ask respondents to nominate their second preference before applying the preference flows at the previous election.

Assuming a uniform swing, for the Labor opposition to get to 76 seats and [[majority government]] would require at least 50.5 percent of the two-party vote (a 4.0-point two-party swing or greater), while for the incumbent Coalition to lose majority government would require 50.2 percent of the two-party vote (a 3.3-point two-party swing or greater).<ref>[[Mackerras pendulum for the Australian federal election, 2016]] based on [http://blogs.abc.net.au/antonygreen/2016/03/2016-federal-election-pendulum-update.html 2016 Federal Election Pendulum (Update): Antony Green ABC 13 March 2016] – O'Connor and Durack not included for Labor majority calculation but are included for Coalition loss of majority calculation.</ref>

